category: science

entry absorbed-dose
term: Absorbed Dose
difficulty: intermediate
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Absorbed dose.webp
related_terms: gray
related_terms: sievert
related_terms: exposure
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
related_terms: dosimeter
quick_facts: 1 Gy = 1 joule of energy per kilogram
quick_facts: Applies to any material, not just living tissue
quick_facts: Different from equivalent dose, which is measured in sieverts
quick_facts: A short whole-body dose of ~5 Gy is often fatal without medical care
quick_facts: Measured with dosimeters and ionization chambers

sources:
    {"organization":"IAEA","title":"Radiation Protection","url":"https://www.iaea.org/topics/radiation-protection"}

definition:
    The amount of radiation energy deposited in matter per unit mass, measured in gray (Gy) — where 1 Gy equals 1 joule of energy absorbed by 1 kilogram of material. It applies to any absorbing material, but in health physics it usually describes energy absorbed by body tissue. It differs from equivalent dose (measured in sieverts), which weights each radiation type by its biological effect.

debunking_snippet:
    Absorbed dose alone doesn't tell you the biological risk. A gray of alpha radiation causes far more tissue damage than a gray of X-rays — that's why equivalent dose (sieverts) exists.
